<UserControl x:Class="Bws.Gui.FilterRow"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             Focusable="False"
             IsTabStop="False">

    <!--
        HOW THE WINDOW ASKS - the chips, their group labels and the button saying which columns the
        list shows. It stood in MainWindow.xaml until 2026-08-13, moved into the theme as a style
        carrying a template the same day (backlog 182), and became a UserControl the day after
        (backlog 185) once the details panel showed what the right shape was.

        THE SEAM IS A SUBJECT RATHER THAN A LINE COUNT: this row is how the window ASKS, and the
        rest of MainWindow.xaml is what the window IS.

        WHY IT IS A UserControl AND NOT A TEMPLATE, WHICH IS THE OWNER'S DECISION OF 2026-08-13
        AFTER THE PANEL MADE THE CHOICE VISIBLE. A theme file has no code-behind class, so a button
        living there had to be found by name at construction, refused with an exception when the
        part was missing, and have its click taken by the window - three mechanisms for one button.
        A UserControl has a class of its own, so the button is a field and the click is a handler,
        and the theme gets its hundred and fifty lines back. One mechanism for "a part of the window
        in its own file" rather than two that have to be told apart.

        Focusable and IsTabStop are off because a UserControl IS a ContentControl and a
        ContentControl is BOTH BY DEFAULT - measured, not assumed. Without them this row is a tab
        stop that does nothing, in front of the chips it holds. `docs/10` trap 8.

        THE CLICKABLE FILTERS, A5. Each one stands for one member of the query, and clicking it
        writes that member into the box above - which is the promise: the filter you clicked is a
        query you can read, edit and learn from. The tooltip shows the member before the click
        rather than after, so the box is not the only place it appears.

        AN ItemsControl RATHER THAN EIGHT CONTROLS WRITTEN OUT. The chips are data - which ones
        there are is decided in FilterChips.cs, where the reason for each is written beside it - and
        markup listing them again would be a second copy that drifts. It also means the row that
        account and signature chips will join is already built.

        THE CHIPS OF A GROUP WRAP INSIDE THEIR ROW, and since 2026-09-15 every group has a row of
        its own - the comment at the ItemsControl below says why. A narrow window folds a group
        onto more lines instead of clipping it. The count line beside the search box is the only
        thing in this window allowed to be cut, because it carries a message of the system's own
        length.

        THE TWO AutomationId VALUES BELOW ARE ON THE CONTROLS, NOT ON THE GRID, AND THAT IS A
        MEASURED CORRECTION. An id was put on the Grid first, and automation never saw it: WPF
        builds a peer for controls, not for layout panels, so the identifier sat on an element that
        is not in the tree at all. tools/window-journey needs to tell the labels of controls apart
        from the sentences the window says about the answer, and it does that by identity rather
        than by where things sit - the same lesson that file has now learnt three times.
    -->

    <Grid>
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <!--
            THE ROW FOLDS AWAY SINCE 2026-08-25, AND THE NUMBER IS WHY. Measured with
            layout-snapshot.ps1 on a window 1650 by 1050: the first column heading stood at 409
            pixels and the first row at 457, so 43 per cent of the window was everything except
            the list. Four wrapped lines of chips are most of it.

            OPEN WHEN THE WINDOW OPENS, and that half is deliberate: complaint 7 of the eleven was
            closed by making the filters visible, and a row that starts folded would reopen it for
            everybody who has never seen this window. What folding costs is nothing at all, because
            the chips are not the only place their state shows - each one writes its member into the
            box above, so a folded row still has "status:stopped" on screen in the query.

            NOT REMEMBERED BETWEEN RUNS, said rather than left to be discovered. The one file this
            program keeps is a frozen contract at schema 2, and a new field in it is a schema bump
            and a conversation rather than an edit.
        -->
        <!--
            AND IT STOPPED WEARING THE ACCENT ON 2026-09-01, WHICH IS A FAULT THIS SESSION MADE AND
            THEN SAW ON A PHOTOGRAPH OF ITS OWN CHANGE.

            The chips beside it became pills that afternoon, so that a filter would stop being drawn
            as the same rounded rectangle as the button that opens a plan to stop a service. That
            repair worked and left this control as the one square, accented, forty-seven pixel tall
            thing in a row of quiet pills - the loudest colour in the whole window, spent on a
            disclosure toggle.

            AND SPENT SAYING SOMETHING THE SCREEN ALREADY SAYS. What the accent announces is that the
            filters are showing, which anybody can see by the filters showing. `docs/11` names this
            shape twice already - the most important state carried in the least visible difference is
            complaint 1, and its mirror is a difference nobody needs carried in the loudest one.

            WHAT IT BECOMES IS THE FAMILY IT BELONGS TO, not a third invention: the quiet button, the
            same as Columns and Show every instance beside it. Those three are the controls that
            change WHAT THE LIST SHOWS rather than what the query asks - which is the boundary
            `docs/11` section 2.9 already drew for where each of them stands.

            THE STYLE IS QuietToggle IN Themes/Chips.xaml SINCE 2026-09-16 - it stood inline here
            until that day, against GUI rule 1, and inline is where its fault hid: the one trigger
            painting the unchecked brushes while checked outranked the library's hover and pressed
            triggers, so the button answered the pointer with nothing for as long as the filters
            were open. The whole argument, and the measurement, are beside the style.
        -->
        <ToggleButton Grid.Column="0"
                      x:Name="FiltersToggle"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="filtersToggle"
                      AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource gui.filter.toggle}"
                      ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.filter.toggle.hint}"
                      Style="{StaticResource QuietToggle}"
                      Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                      VerticalAlignment="Top"
                      IsChecked="True">
            <!--
                AutomationProperties.Name IS ABOVE BECAUSE THE CONTENT STOPPED BEING A STRING. A
                ContentControl handed a panel has no name of its own, so without that line the one
                control folding this whole row away goes into the tree unnamed - and the three
                instruments that find controls by identity would still see the id and report a
                nameless button, which reads like a fault somewhere else.
            -->
            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                <Path Style="{StaticResource FilterDisclosure}" />

                <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource gui.filter.toggle}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </StackPanel>
        </ToggleButton>

        <!--
            ONE GROUP PER ROW, WITH THE LABELS IN ONE COLUMN, SINCE 2026-09-15 - point 7 of the
            review in `docs/11` 2.14, the owner's decision with the price named. The groups used to
            flow in a WrapPanel, so the fifth label landed in the middle of the fourth row beside
            the chips of the fourth group - a form whose labels are not a column, which GUI rule 13
            forbids. A vertical panel (the ItemsControl's default) gives every group its own row
            and a finite width - `docs/10` trap 18 the right way round - and the shared size scope
            below is what lines the five labels up: FilterGroupTemplate puts each one in a column
            named FilterLabel, and a scope on this control makes those five columns one width.

            THE PRICE, SAID BEFORE IT WAS PAID AND MEASURED AFTER: a fifth row of chips. The
            estimate was 41 pixels, and layout-snapshot.ps1 at 1650 by 1050 device pixels read the
            column headings moving from y 445 to 492 - 47 device pixels, about 31 logical at this
            machine's 150 per cent, one row of the list. `docs/11` 2.12 keeps the height of this
            block as the owner's open question, and the fold button beside it is the answer that
            already exists. The cheaper repair was rejected as WRONG rather than
            ugly: moving Trigger-started into the Startup type group would make it read as one of
            the start types, and chips in one group mean OR where chips across groups mean AND.
        -->
        <ItemsControl Grid.Column="1"
                      x:Name="ChipRow"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="filterChips"
                      Grid.IsSharedSizeScope="True"
                      ItemsSource="{Binding FilterGroups}"
                      ItemTemplate="{StaticResource FilterGroupTemplate}">
            <ItemsControl.Style>
                <Style TargetType="ItemsControl">
                    <Style.Triggers>
                        <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding IsChecked, ElementName=FiltersToggle}" Value="False">
                            <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                        </DataTrigger>
                    </Style.Triggers>
                </Style>
            </ItemsControl.Style>
        </ItemsControl>

        <!--
            WHETHER EVERY SESSION'S COPY IS ON SCREEN, `A11`. Windows makes one per-user copy per
            logged-on session with a hex tail on the name, and the specification's whole complaint
            about them is that they are noise - measured on this machine 2026-08-25, 23 of 798
            entries, and that many times over on a machine with several people logged on.

            AT THIS END OF THE ROW RATHER THAN AMONG THE CHIPS, and the argument is the one written
            over the Columns button below and over the whole of ScopeBar.xaml: what it changes is
            the LIST rather than the question. A filter chip writes what it stands for into the
            search box, so somebody can read it, edit it and learn the language from it - this
            writes nothing there and could not, because there is no member of the query language
            that means "draw these two rows as one". Standing it among the chips would say the
            opposite of what it does, which is the mistake the drivers chip was making for a week.

            IT IS NOT A FILTER AND THE COUNT LINE IS WHERE THAT SHOWS. The number above the list
            goes on counting entries the query selected, and folding makes the list shorter than
            that number - so the window owes a sentence naming the difference and the way out of
            it. Sentences.Admissions carries it, beside the other things an answer admits.

            A ToggleButton with no style of its own, exactly like the Filters button at the other
            end: this row already dresses two-state controls that way, and `ADR-23` is about not
            inventing a third look for the same kind of thing.

            NOT REMEMBERED BETWEEN RUNS, the same sentence the Filters button carries. The one file
            this program keeps is a frozen contract, and a new field in it is a schema bump and a
            conversation rather than an edit.

            Its own AutomationId, because tools/window-journey tells controls apart by identity
            rather than by where they sit - the fifth time this row has paid for that lesson.
        -->
        <ToggleButton Grid.Column="2"
                      x:Name="InstancesToggle"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="instancesToggle"
                      Content="{DynamicResource gui.instances.toggle}"
                      ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.instances.toggle.hint}"
                      Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                      VerticalAlignment="Top"
                      IsChecked="{Binding ShowingEveryInstance, Mode=TwoWay}" />

        <!--
            WHICH COLUMNS THE LIST SHOWS, `A8`. A VISIBLE CONTROL RATHER THAN A MENU HIDDEN UNDER
            THE COLUMN HEADINGS, and that is `docs/11` rather than taste: section 6 grades
            "recognition rather than recall" as one of this window's two weakest heuristics, and a
            feature reachable only by right clicking something is a feature you have to already
            know about. The filters made the same choice one slice earlier - complaint 7 was closed
            with a visible row and a group label, not with a menu.

            The list ITSELF is a menu, which is the opposite decision and is about surface rather
            than discoverability: a panel of our own would need a fill, an edge and both of their
            contrast ratios argued, for a thing somebody opens a few times. The theme already
            dresses a menu and this window already uses one to copy a name.

            It sits at the end of the filters row rather than beside the search box, because what it
            changes is the list rather than the question - and the count line, which answers the
            question, already owns the right-hand end of the row above.

            Its own AutomationId, because tools/window-journey tells the labels of controls apart
            from the sentences the window says about the answer, and it does that by identity. This
            is the fourth time that lesson has been paid for in this row.
        -->
        <!--
            VerticalAlignment SINCE 2026-09-02, AND WITHOUT IT THESE TWO WERE ON DIFFERENT LINES
            WHILE SITTING IN THE SAME GRID ROW. Show every instance carried Top and this did not, so
            the library's own Center took over - and a row four chips deep centres a control 70
            points below its neighbour. Measured off `layout-baseline.json`: 185 against 255, with
            their right edges 125 apart as well. The owner's words were that they look randomly
            placed, and two controls of the same family at two heights and two right edges are
            exactly that.

            THE PAIR IS RIGHT ALIGNED AS A PAIR: this one sits on the window's content edge and
            Show every instance sits against it, eight points away, both on the first chip row.
        -->
        <Button Grid.Column="3"
                x:Name="ColumnsButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="columnsButton"
                Content="{DynamicResource gui.columns.button}"
                ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.columns.hint}"
                VerticalAlignment="Top"
                Click="ChooseColumns">
            <Button.ContextMenu>
                <!--
                    A CEILING AND A SCROLLBAR, because eighteen items is taller than a short screen
                    and a menu that runs off the bottom loses the last of them with nothing saying
                    so. The headings come from a flat list built in the window - see its
                    constructor.
                -->
                <ContextMenu MaxHeight="{StaticResource HeightMenu}"
                             ScrollViewer.VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" />
            </Button.ContextMenu>
        </Button>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
